1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:04,880
In 1965, a chemist named Gordon Moore

2
00:00:04,920 --> 00:00:07,240
drew a line through a handful of dots,

3
00:00:07,280 --> 00:00:10,370
and made a guess even he thought was a stretch.

4
00:00:10,480 --> 00:00:13,000
Each dot showed how many parts engineers

5
00:00:13,040 --> 00:00:15,490
could squeeze onto a single chip.

6
00:00:15,600 --> 00:00:17,480
That number had been doubling.

7
00:00:17,520 --> 00:00:19,050
Roughly every year.

8
00:00:19,440 --> 00:00:22,810
So Moore kept the line going, ten more years.

9
00:00:23,648 --> 00:00:25,240
It's been sixty.

10
00:00:25,280 --> 00:00:27,640
The biggest AI chip announced this year

11
00:00:27,680 --> 00:00:30,890
holds more than 300 billion of those parts.

12
00:00:31,200 --> 00:00:33,080
Chips like that are why the AI

13
00:00:33,120 --> 00:00:35,130
you talk to exists at all.

14
00:00:35,968 --> 00:00:38,810
And now everyone says we're at the turning point.

15
00:00:38,960 --> 00:00:41,610
The moment the curve bends upward.

16
00:00:41,968 --> 00:00:44,810
If we really were, how would we know?

17
00:00:49,760 --> 00:00:52,520
Most of us picture progress as a ramp.

18
00:00:52,560 --> 00:00:54,410
A little better every year.

19
00:00:54,880 --> 00:00:58,680
In 1975, two Dutch researchers asked people

20
00:00:58,720 --> 00:01:01,610
to guess where a doubling process ends up.

21
00:01:01,840 --> 00:01:03,800
Often two thirds of them guessed

22
00:01:03,840 --> 00:01:06,280
less than a tenth of the real answer.

23
00:01:06,320 --> 00:01:08,760
Even when they were told how doubling works,

24
00:01:08,800 --> 00:01:10,010
it barely helped.

25
00:01:10,240 --> 00:01:13,050
And it doesn't only fool ordinary people.

26
00:01:14,300 --> 00:01:17,220
Every year, the International Energy Agency

27
00:01:17,260 --> 00:01:20,470
publishes a big outlook on the world's energy.

28
00:01:20,540 --> 00:01:21,860
For more than a decade,

29
00:01:21,900 --> 00:01:23,820
it kept showing new solar power

30
00:01:23,860 --> 00:01:25,620
each year growing for a bit,

31
00:01:25,660 --> 00:01:27,460
and then levelling off.

32
00:01:27,500 --> 00:01:30,990
And every year, more solar got built than that.

33
00:01:31,100 --> 00:01:32,980
They drew a flat line.

34
00:01:33,020 --> 00:01:34,870
Reality bent upward.

35
00:01:35,260 --> 00:01:38,070
So the next year they drew another flat line.

36
00:01:39,100 --> 00:01:41,380
Solar runs on a loop.

37
00:01:41,420 --> 00:01:43,620
Every time the world doubles the number of solar

38
00:01:43,660 --> 00:01:45,380
panels it has ever made,

39
00:01:45,420 --> 00:01:47,830
they get about 20% cheaper.

40
00:01:47,900 --> 00:01:50,070
Cheaper panels mean more buyers.

41
00:01:50,300 --> 00:01:52,660
More buyers mean more panels.

42
00:01:52,700 --> 00:01:55,350
The result feeds its own cause.

43
00:01:55,420 --> 00:01:57,910
That's what makes growth compound.

44
00:01:57,980 --> 00:02:00,180
Since the seventies, the price of a solar

45
00:02:00,220 --> 00:02:03,590
panel has fallen by more than 99%.

46
00:02:04,840 --> 00:02:07,360
AI is running on loops like that.

47
00:02:07,400 --> 00:02:09,170
Several of them, stacked.

48
00:02:09,560 --> 00:02:12,320
The computing power used to train the leading AI

49
00:02:12,360 --> 00:02:14,320
models has grown four to five

50
00:02:14,360 --> 00:02:16,720
times a year since 2010.

51
00:02:16,760 --> 00:02:19,520
Running them gets cheaper even faster.

52
00:02:19,560 --> 00:02:22,640
On one test of PhD-level science questions,

53
00:02:22,680 --> 00:02:23,520
the same quality

54
00:02:23,560 --> 00:02:25,360
of answer has been getting about

55
00:02:25,400 --> 00:02:27,490
forty times cheaper per year.

56
00:02:27,800 --> 00:02:30,360
And the tasks AI can finish on its own,

57
00:02:30,400 --> 00:02:33,250
at least half the time, keep getting longer.

58
00:02:33,320 --> 00:02:35,600
Measured in how long they'd take a person,

59
00:02:35,640 --> 00:02:36,800
that length has doubled

60
00:02:36,840 --> 00:02:38,880
roughly every seven months.

61
00:02:38,920 --> 00:02:41,090
Lately it may be speeding up.

62
00:02:42,340 --> 00:02:44,380
So where's the turning point?

63
00:02:44,420 --> 00:02:46,430
Where does the curve bend?

64
00:02:46,500 --> 00:02:49,260
Draw any of these numbers on an ordinary chart,

65
00:02:49,300 --> 00:02:50,750
and you get a hockey stick.

66
00:02:50,980 --> 00:02:53,390
Flat for years, then straight up.

67
00:02:53,540 --> 00:02:54,670
Right at the end.

68
00:02:55,060 --> 00:02:57,820
Now cut the chart off five years earlier.

69
00:02:57,860 --> 00:02:58,860
Same shape.

70
00:02:58,900 --> 00:02:59,980
Same wall.

71
00:03:00,020 --> 00:03:01,390
Right at the new end.

72
00:03:02,180 --> 00:03:03,660
On a true exponential,

73
00:03:03,700 --> 00:03:05,740
that bend isn't in the curve.

74
00:03:05,780 --> 00:03:07,100
It's in the chart.

75
00:03:07,140 --> 00:03:10,030
It shows up wherever you happen to cut it off.

76
00:03:10,100 --> 00:03:11,700
Stand in 1990,

77
00:03:11,740 --> 00:03:13,820
and everything behind you looks flat,

78
00:03:13,860 --> 00:03:16,460
and the next few years look like a wall.

79
00:03:16,500 --> 00:03:17,950
Stand in 2010.

80
00:03:18,100 --> 00:03:19,100
Same thing.

81
00:03:19,220 --> 00:03:20,380
Stand here.

82
00:03:20,420 --> 00:03:21,420
Same thing.

83
00:03:21,620 --> 00:03:24,310
Behind you, it always looks flat.

84
00:03:24,420 --> 00:03:27,070
So everyone who lives on a curve like this

85
00:03:27,140 --> 00:03:29,340
feels like they're at the turning point.

86
00:03:29,380 --> 00:03:31,300
And that feeling, on its own,

87
00:03:31,340 --> 00:03:32,990
tells you almost nothing.

88
00:03:33,900 --> 00:03:36,420
What does tell you something is speed.

89
00:03:36,460 --> 00:03:38,220
How fast is it doubling?

90
00:03:38,260 --> 00:03:41,230
And how many doublings does it take us to react?

91
00:03:42,480 --> 00:03:44,730
Now, the skeptics have a point.

92
00:03:44,960 --> 00:03:47,290
No real curve doubles forever.

93
00:03:47,600 --> 00:03:49,800
Bacteria run out of food.

94
00:03:49,840 --> 00:03:52,240
Moore himself had to slow his line down,

95
00:03:52,280 --> 00:03:54,640
to a doubling every two years.

96
00:03:54,680 --> 00:03:56,440
AI could run short of fresh

97
00:03:56,480 --> 00:03:58,360
human writing to learn from.

98
00:03:58,400 --> 00:04:01,080
Researchers at Epoch AI think the supply

99
00:04:01,120 --> 00:04:05,080
could be used up anytime between now and 2032.

100
00:04:05,120 --> 00:04:07,160
Even solar is cooling off.

101
00:04:07,200 --> 00:04:08,720
Global installations grew

102
00:04:08,760 --> 00:04:11,187
by about a third in 2024,

103
00:04:11,227 --> 00:04:13,640
and only about a tenth last year.

104
00:04:13,680 --> 00:04:15,720
In nature, every exponential

105
00:04:15,760 --> 00:04:18,280
turns into an S-curve eventually.

106
00:04:18,320 --> 00:04:20,703
Nobody knows where the top of this one is.

107
00:04:21,680 --> 00:04:24,680
The solar forecasters thought they knew, too.

108
00:04:24,720 --> 00:04:26,760
And even a curve that's slowing down

109
00:04:26,800 --> 00:04:29,770
can carry us a long way before it stops.

110
00:04:31,020 --> 00:04:32,310
So try this.

111
00:04:32,700 --> 00:04:36,100
Something in your life doubles every seven months.

112
00:04:36,140 --> 00:04:37,857
When do you start getting ready?

113
00:04:38,940 --> 00:04:41,860
Most of us would say, once it gets big.

114
00:04:41,900 --> 00:04:43,540
But run it backwards.

115
00:04:43,580 --> 00:04:47,220
Seven months before it's everywhere, it's at half.

116
00:04:47,260 --> 00:04:50,140
Fourteen months before, a quarter.

117
00:04:50,180 --> 00:04:53,620
About four years before, it's under 1%.

118
00:04:53,660 --> 00:04:54,977
It looks like nothing.

119
00:04:56,020 --> 00:04:57,700
So that's the fork.

120
00:04:57,740 --> 00:04:59,780
Either we wait until it's obvious,

121
00:04:59,820 --> 00:05:02,150
or we act on the doubling time.

122
00:05:03,400 --> 00:05:06,080
Let's imagine it's 2035.

123
00:05:06,120 --> 00:05:07,330
Just a scenario.

124
00:05:07,560 --> 00:05:11,210
A student in Lahore sits at a desk by a window.

125
00:05:11,400 --> 00:05:13,760
In one version, we waited.

126
00:05:13,800 --> 00:05:17,360
Each new jump in AI arrived as a surprise.

127
00:05:17,400 --> 00:05:18,880
The rules came later,

128
00:05:18,920 --> 00:05:21,730
written for the world of two doublings ago.

129
00:05:21,800 --> 00:05:24,050
Her school banned the new tools.

130
00:05:24,280 --> 00:05:26,160
She picked them up two years late,

131
00:05:26,200 --> 00:05:27,850
from a cousin's phone.

132
00:05:27,960 --> 00:05:29,280
Nothing exploded.

133
00:05:29,320 --> 00:05:31,680
We just kept being caught off guard,

134
00:05:31,720 --> 00:05:32,720
and each surprise

135
00:05:32,760 --> 00:05:34,560
landed hardest on whoever had

136
00:05:34,600 --> 00:05:36,317
the least time to prepare.

137
00:05:37,200 --> 00:05:38,160
Same desk.

138
00:05:38,200 --> 00:05:39,360
Same year.

139
00:05:39,400 --> 00:05:40,530
The other version.

140
00:05:41,080 --> 00:05:44,160
She learns from an AI tutor on a cheap phone,

141
00:05:44,200 --> 00:05:47,120
charged by a panel on her family's roof.

142
00:05:47,160 --> 00:05:49,760
Her doctor's newest antibiotic was designed

143
00:05:49,800 --> 00:05:53,360
with help from a map of proteins that an AI drew.

144
00:05:53,400 --> 00:05:55,013
And when an AI model crossed

145
00:05:55,053 --> 00:05:57,040
a danger line last spring,

146
00:05:57,080 --> 00:05:58,720
its extra safeguards switched

147
00:05:58,760 --> 00:06:01,437
on before anyone had to argue about it.

148
00:06:02,280 --> 00:06:04,210
Look at what's the same in both.

149
00:06:04,520 --> 00:06:05,520
Same chips.

150
00:06:05,560 --> 00:06:06,560
Same curve.

151
00:06:06,600 --> 00:06:07,650
Same speed.

152
00:06:07,880 --> 00:06:09,760
The only thing that changed is when

153
00:06:09,800 --> 00:06:12,050
people started paying attention.

154
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:15,690
So how do we get there?

155
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:18,520
Start with the cheapest fix there is.

156
00:06:18,560 --> 00:06:20,330
Learn to read the curve.

157
00:06:20,800 --> 00:06:22,360
In March 2020,

158
00:06:22,400 --> 00:06:25,160
as the pandemic began, psychologists found

159
00:06:25,200 --> 00:06:26,920
that most people pictured the virus

160
00:06:26,960 --> 00:06:29,050
spreading in a straight line.

161
00:06:29,120 --> 00:06:30,680
Then they walked some of them through

162
00:06:30,720 --> 00:06:33,530
how doubling works, step by step.

163
00:06:33,600 --> 00:06:35,370
Their guesses got better.

164
00:06:35,440 --> 00:06:36,760
And they backed the measures

165
00:06:36,800 --> 00:06:39,130
to slow the spread more strongly.

166
00:06:39,280 --> 00:06:40,520
Back in the seventies,

167
00:06:40,560 --> 00:06:42,920
a quick instruction barely helped.

168
00:06:42,960 --> 00:06:46,000
Working through the doublings yourself seems to.

169
00:06:46,040 --> 00:06:48,400
And knowing still isn't doing.

170
00:06:48,440 --> 00:06:50,600
But it costs almost nothing.

171
00:06:50,640 --> 00:06:53,560
So next time you hear a number about AI,

172
00:06:53,600 --> 00:06:55,160
ask one question.

173
00:06:55,200 --> 00:06:57,290
How long does it take to double?

174
00:06:58,340 --> 00:07:01,180
But reading the curve doesn't stop anything.

175
00:07:01,220 --> 00:07:03,660
For that you need brakes, and they should

176
00:07:03,700 --> 00:07:06,630
be tied to the curve, not the calendar.

177
00:07:06,820 --> 00:07:10,140
In 2024, sixteen AI companies met

178
00:07:10,180 --> 00:07:13,220
in Seoul and signed voluntary commitments.

179
00:07:13,260 --> 00:07:14,620
Each would publish its own

180
00:07:14,660 --> 00:07:16,460
danger lines in advance.

181
00:07:16,500 --> 00:07:17,700
And in the extreme,

182
00:07:17,740 --> 00:07:19,940
if it couldn't keep a model's risks below

183
00:07:19,980 --> 00:07:21,740
those lines, it wouldn't build

184
00:07:21,780 --> 00:07:24,140
or release that model at all.

185
00:07:24,180 --> 00:07:25,980
In May 2025,

186
00:07:26,020 --> 00:07:28,820
Anthropic was about to release a new model.

187
00:07:28,860 --> 00:07:31,100
For the first time, its tests couldn't

188
00:07:31,140 --> 00:07:32,700
rule out that the model might give

189
00:07:32,740 --> 00:07:34,620
real help to someone trying to build

190
00:07:34,660 --> 00:07:37,180
a chemical or biological weapon.

191
00:07:37,220 --> 00:07:39,500
It hadn't proven the line was crossed.

192
00:07:39,540 --> 00:07:41,820
It just couldn't rule it out anymore.

193
00:07:41,860 --> 00:07:44,780
So it launched with stricter safeguards anyway,

194
00:07:44,820 --> 00:07:46,340
as a precaution.

195
00:07:46,380 --> 00:07:48,380
A tripwire like that doesn't care

196
00:07:48,420 --> 00:07:50,460
how fast the curve is moving.

197
00:07:50,500 --> 00:07:53,260
It goes off when the measurement says so.

198
00:07:53,300 --> 00:07:54,780
The catch is that the companies

199
00:07:54,820 --> 00:07:56,540
mostly grade themselves,

200
00:07:56,580 --> 00:07:58,910
and they can rewrite their own rules.

201
00:07:58,980 --> 00:08:01,500
Anthropic did, early this year.

202
00:08:01,540 --> 00:08:03,180
It dropped its promise to pause

203
00:08:03,220 --> 00:08:05,180
if its safeguards weren't ready,

204
00:08:05,220 --> 00:08:08,590
arguing that one lab can't hold the line alone.

205
00:08:08,660 --> 00:08:11,100
Critics called it a broken promise.

206
00:08:11,140 --> 00:08:13,980
Which is why we think tripwires belong in law,

207
00:08:14,020 --> 00:08:16,590
tied to measurements instead of dates.

208
00:08:17,640 --> 00:08:20,530
Brakes only tell the curve where not to go.

209
00:08:20,840 --> 00:08:22,450
Where should it go?

210
00:08:22,680 --> 00:08:26,160
For fifty years, working out how a protein folds

211
00:08:26,200 --> 00:08:29,570
was one of biology's great unsolved problems.

212
00:08:29,640 --> 00:08:32,240
Then an AI called AlphaFold

213
00:08:32,280 --> 00:08:34,000
predicted the structures of virtually

214
00:08:34,040 --> 00:08:36,400
all of the 200 million proteins

215
00:08:36,440 --> 00:08:38,160
researchers know about.

216
00:08:38,200 --> 00:08:41,360
Two of its makers shared a Nobel Prize for it.

217
00:08:41,400 --> 00:08:43,520
More than 2 million people in a hundred

218
00:08:43,560 --> 00:08:46,050
and ninety countries have used that map.

219
00:08:46,440 --> 00:08:48,240
A map isn't a medicine.

220
00:08:48,280 --> 00:08:50,800
Most of the slow work is still ahead.

221
00:08:50,840 --> 00:08:51,840
But it's a good question

222
00:08:51,880 --> 00:08:54,210
for any field, including yours.

223
00:08:54,520 --> 00:08:57,410
Which problem is still waiting for its map?

224
00:08:58,460 --> 00:08:59,700
And then there's the question

225
00:08:59,740 --> 00:09:02,180
of who gets to ride the curve.

226
00:09:02,220 --> 00:09:03,670
Remember the solar loop?

227
00:09:03,980 --> 00:09:07,060
In 2024, Pakistan imported about

228
00:09:07,100 --> 00:09:09,620
17 gigawatts of solar panels.

229
00:09:09,660 --> 00:09:11,940
Twice as much as the year before.

230
00:09:11,980 --> 00:09:13,620
Mostly for rooftops.

231
00:09:13,660 --> 00:09:15,620
Shops, offices, families

232
00:09:15,660 --> 00:09:17,750
escaping high power bills.

233
00:09:17,820 --> 00:09:19,780
Nobody planned it from above.

234
00:09:19,820 --> 00:09:22,950
Panels got cheap enough, and people did the math.

235
00:09:23,020 --> 00:09:24,220
It's messy.

236
00:09:24,260 --> 00:09:26,860
The national grid now sells less power,

237
00:09:26,900 --> 00:09:27,540
and the government

238
00:09:27,580 --> 00:09:28,980
has been rethinking the deal

239
00:09:29,020 --> 00:09:30,860
it gives rooftop owners.

240
00:09:30,900 --> 00:09:32,940
And a panel still needs a roof,

241
00:09:32,980 --> 00:09:34,780
and the money to buy it.

242
00:09:34,820 --> 00:09:38,500
AI is getting cheaper even faster than solar did.

243
00:09:38,540 --> 00:09:40,980
Who gets it first, and on what terms,

244
00:09:41,020 --> 00:09:42,820
hasn't been decided yet.

245
00:09:42,860 --> 00:09:45,110
That part is still up to us.

246
00:09:46,860 --> 00:09:49,940
Back to that desk in 1965.

247
00:09:49,980 --> 00:09:52,980
Moore didn't see the future by guessing big.

248
00:09:53,020 --> 00:09:55,060
He measured something carefully,

249
00:09:55,100 --> 00:09:57,830
and then he took his own numbers seriously.

250
00:09:58,140 --> 00:09:59,700
We can do the same.

251
00:09:59,740 --> 00:10:01,750
Ask how fast it's doubling.

252
00:10:01,980 --> 00:10:03,780
Try the tools in your own field

253
00:10:03,820 --> 00:10:05,620
while it still looks early.

254
00:10:05,660 --> 00:10:07,460
And ask the people who represent

255
00:10:07,500 --> 00:10:10,150
you to turn the tripwires into law.

256
00:10:11,020 --> 00:10:14,020
One day, from somewhere further up this curve,

257
00:10:14,060 --> 00:10:16,550
everything behind us will look flat.

258
00:10:16,620 --> 00:10:18,510
Today will look flat.

259
00:10:18,620 --> 00:10:19,780
Let's make sure that what's

260
00:10:19,820 --> 00:10:22,470
ahead is something we chose.

261
00:10:22,780 --> 00:10:24,260
What's one thing you've watched

262
00:10:24,300 --> 00:10:26,740
go from "never" to "everywhere"?

263
00:10:26,780 --> 00:10:28,230
Tell me in the comments.

